body:
## Summary

Two hyperelastic blocks with nonmatching Hex8 meshes are pressed together through a frictionless contact pair. The exact solution is a uniform affine deformation that every mesh represents exactly. The two coarsest meshes reach the full load and match it to ≤ 1.3e-6 m. **Every finer mesh tried (n = 3, 4, 5) stops partway**:
- n = 3 (75 elements) stops at load factor 0.890836;
- n = 4 at 0.625434;
- n = 5 at 0.525576.

Line search does not help. It moves n = 3 to 0.963295 but n = 4 down to 0.192072, and 100 iterations change nothing.

Tested revision: `cb976117889edf8de5f093ba949980b0aa79c1e2`, rustc 1.99.1. The n = 1–3 results (all stopping factors and residuals) are identical at `afb75430fa0cf6fb1b8e707eea9d395a563d4e0f`.

## Problem and exact solution

- **Geometry:** a lower block [0,1]×[0,1]×[−0.5,0] m and an upper block [0,1]×[0,1]×[0,0.5] m, touching at z = 0 with no initial gap or overlap.
- **Mesh:** Hex8 with full displacement interpolation. The lower block is n×n×n and the upper (n+1)×(n+1)×n, so the interface is nonmatching. The blocks share no nodes.
- **Material:** the same compressible neo-Hookean (`hyperelastic: neo_hookean`) for both blocks, E = 1 MPa, ν = 0.3 (μ = 384 615.38 Pa, K = 833 333.33 Pa), energy W = μ/2 (Ī₁ − 3) + K/2 (J − 1)². Density 1000 kg/m³ is irrelevant (static).
- **Contact:** one `pair` contact, main = lower top face, secondary = upper bottom face, frictionless (`friction.static = 0`), `initial_penetration: refuse`, `penalty_scale: 1`.
- **Boundary conditions:** u_x = 0 on x = 0, u_y = 0 on y = 0, u_z = 0 on the bottom z = −0.5. The top z = 0.5 is ramped to u_z = −0.1 m. All other components are free.
- **Solver:** nonlinear geometry; increments 0.05 (initial and maximum), minimum 1e-8, at most 1000 increments and 30 iterations. Tolerances: force 1e-6, displacement 1e-5, energy 1e-10.

The exact solution is the homogeneous deformation F = diag(a, a, 0.9), i.e. u = ((a−1)x, (a−1)y, −0.1(z+0.5)). The lateral stretch a makes the lateral Cauchy stress vanish. With σ = μ J^(−5/3) (b − tr(b)/3 · I) + K(J − 1) I, the condition is

μ J^(−5/3) (a² − 0.81)/3 + K (J − 1) = 0,  with J = 0.9 a²,

which gives **a = 1.0316962805**, J = 0.9579574937, σ_zz = −105 106.27 Pa, zero lateral and shear stress, and a normal resultant of σ_zz · a² · 1 m² = **−111 874.82 N**. Every Hex8 mesh represents this affine field exactly, and the interface stays planar, so the contact constraint is met exactly with zero gap. Only finite penalty enforcement permits small deviations.

## Reproduce

A 100-line program using only the public `fieldcraft-model` / `fieldcraft-solver` API. It generates both meshes, solves, and compares the result with the exact field.

`Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[package]
name = "contact-compression"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2024"

[workspace]

[dependencies]
fieldcraft-model = { git = "https://github.com/tangency-labs/fieldcraft", rev = "cb976117889edf8de5f093ba949980b0aa79c1e2" }
fieldcraft-solver = { git = "https://github.com/tangency-labs/fieldcraft", rev = "cb976117889edf8de5f093ba949980b0aa79c1e2" }
serde_json = "1"
```

`src/main.rs`:

```rust
//! Two neo-Hookean Hex8 blocks pressed together through a frictionless contact pair.
//! Lower block [0,1]x[0,1]x[-0.5,0] has n x n x n elements; upper block [0,1]x[0,1]x[0,0.5] has
//! (n+1) x (n+1) x n, so the interface is nonmatching. u_x = 0 on x = 0, u_y = 0 on y = 0,
//! u_z = 0 on z = -0.5, and the top z = 0.5 is ramped to u_z = -0.1. The exact solution is the
//! affine field u = ((a-1)x, (a-1)y, -0.1(z+0.5)) with a = 1.0316962805.
//! Usage: contact-compression <n> [line-search] [max-iterations=30]
use fieldcraft_model::*;
use fieldcraft_solver::{Limits, solve_nonlinear};
use serde_json::{from_value, json};

type Res<T> = std::result::Result<T, Box<dyn std::error::Error>>;
const A: f64 = 1.0316962805;

/// Adds an m x m x n Hex8 block over z in [z0, z0 + 0.5]; returns its top (face 2) or bottom (face 1) faces.
fn block(model: &mut Model, nodes: &mut Vec<[f64; 3]>, elements: &mut u32, m: usize, n: usize, z0: f64,
         top: bool) -> Res<Vec<ElementFace>> {
    let first = nodes.len() as u32 + 1;
    let id = |i: usize, j: usize, k: usize| first + (i + (m + 1) * (j + (m + 1) * k)) as u32;
    for k in 0..=n {
        for j in 0..=m {
            for i in 0..=m {
                nodes.push([i as f64 / m as f64, j as f64 / m as f64, z0 + k as f64 * 0.5 / n as f64]);
                model.set_node(nodes.len() as u32, *nodes.last().unwrap())?;
            }
        }
    }
    let mut faces = Vec::new();
    for k in 0..n {
        for j in 0..m {
            for i in 0..m {
                *elements += 1;
                model.set_element(*elements, ElementKind::Hex8, vec![id(i, j, k), id(i + 1, j, k),
                    id(i + 1, j + 1, k), id(i, j + 1, k), id(i, j, k + 1), id(i + 1, j, k + 1),
                    id(i + 1, j + 1, k + 1), id(i, j + 1, k + 1)])?;
                if (top && k == n - 1) || (!top && k == 0) {
                    faces.push(ElementFace { element: *elements, face: if top { 2 } else { 1 } });
                }
            }
        }
    }
    Ok(faces)
}

fn main() -> Res<()> {
    let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();
    let n: usize = args[1].parse()?;
    let line_search = args.get(2).is_some_and(|a| a == "line-search");
    let iterations: u32 = match args.get(3) { Some(s) => s.parse()?, None => 30 };

    let mut model = Model::default();
    let (mut nodes, mut elements) = (Vec::new(), 0u32);
    let lower = block(&mut model, &mut nodes, &mut elements, n, n, -0.5, true)?;
    let upper = block(&mut model, &mut nodes, &mut elements, n + 1, n, 0.0, false)?;

    model.add_set("all", Set { elements: (1..=elements).collect(), ..Set::default() })?;
    model.add_material("material", Material {
        elastic: Some(Elastic::Isotropic { young: 1e6, poisson: 0.3 }),
        hyperelastic: Some(from_value(json!({"type": "neo_hookean"}))?),
        density: Some(1000.),
        ..Material::default()
    })?;
    model.add_section("section", Section { set: "all".into(), material: "material".into(),
        kind: SectionKind::Solid {}, formulation: Formulation::default() })?;
    model.add_set("lower", Set { faces: lower, ..Set::default() })?;
    model.add_set("upper", Set { faces: upper, ..Set::default() })?;
    model.add_contact("interface", from_value(json!({"friction": {"static": 0.0},
        "initial_penetration": "refuse", "penalty_scale": 1.0,
        "surfaces": {"main": "lower", "secondary": "upper", "type": "pair"}}))?)?;
    model.add_step(Step::new("step", Procedure::NonlinearStatic(from_value(json!({
        "initial_increment": 0.05, "max_increment": 0.05, "min_increment": 1e-8,
        "max_increments": 1000, "max_iterations": iterations, "nonlinear_geometry": true,
        "line_search": line_search,
        "tolerances": {"displacement": 1e-5, "energy": 1e-10, "force": 1e-6}}))?)), None)?;

    for (label, xyz) in (1u32..).zip(&nodes) {
        let mut values = [None; 6];
        if xyz[0] == 0. { values[0] = Some(0.); }
        if xyz[1] == 0. { values[1] = Some(0.); }
        if xyz[2] == -0.5 { values[2] = Some(0.); }
        if xyz[2] == 0.5 { values[2] = Some(-0.1); }
        if values.iter().any(Option::is_some) {
            let name = format!("bc{label}");
            model.add_set(&name, Set { nodes: vec![label], ..Set::default() })?;
            model.add_boundary_condition(&name, BoundaryCondition::Displacement {
                target: Target::Set(name.clone()), values })?;
            model.set_boundary_condition_active("step", &name, true)?;
        }
    }

    let result = solve_nonlinear(&model, Some("step"), &Limits::default())?;
    let mut error = 0f64;
    for (i, label) in result.nodes.iter().enumerate() {
        let [x, y, z] = nodes[*label as usize - 1];
        let exact = [(A - 1.) * x, (A - 1.) * y, -0.1 * (z + 0.5)];
        for d in 0..3 { error = error.max((result.displacement[i][d] - exact[d]).abs()); }
    }
    println!("{} nodes, {elements} elements: full load reached, max nodal error vs exact {error:.2e} m",
        nodes.len());
    Ok(())
}
```

```sh
cargo run --release -- 2                    # passes
cargo run --release -- 3                    # fails
cargo run --release -- 3 line-search        # fails
```

Output:

```text
$ contact-compression 2
75 nodes, 26 elements: full load reached, max nodal error vs exact 1.26e-6 m
$ contact-compression 3
Error: NotConverged("step `step` stopped at load factor 0.890836 (step fraction 0.890836): the increment to 0.890836 failed (no convergence in 30 iterations) and a smaller one would be below the minimum increment 0.00000001")
```

## Expected vs actual

Every row should reach the full load with a nodal error at the penalty-consistent level of the n = 1, 2 rows.

| Arguments | Mesh (lower / upper) | Actual |
|---|---|---|
| `1` | 1×1×1 / 2×2×1 | full load; max nodal error **4.6e-7 m** |
| `2` | 2×2×2 / 3×3×2 | full load; max nodal error **1.3e-6 m** |
| `3` | 3×3×3 / 4×4×3 | **stops at 0.890836**: no convergence in 30 iterations |
| `3 line-search` | same, line search | **stops at 0.963295**: "the residual grows (2.99e-8, 4.89e-8, 5.05e-8 of the force scale)" |
| `3 line-search 100` | same, 100 iterations | same stop at 0.963295 |
| `4` | 4×4×4 / 5×5×4 | **stops at 0.625434**: "the residual grows (1.85e-7, 2.03e-7, 2.04e-7 of the force scale)" |
| `4 line-search` | same, line search | **stops at 0.192072**: no convergence in 30 iterations |
| `5` | 5×5×5 / 6×6×5 | **stops at 0.525576**: no convergence in 30 iterations |

Each stop says the next increment "would be below the minimum increment 0.00000001". The solver has halved the increment down to the 1e-8 floor.

In two of the failing rows, the residuals that "grow" are 3e-8 to 2e-7 of the force scale. That is already below the 1e-6 force tolerance. Increment acceptance must therefore be blocked by one of:
- the displacement criterion;
- the energy criterion;
- the residual-growth rule firing at a level where the force criterion is already met.

It is not blocked by a genuinely diverging iteration. The failure message does not say which criterion rejected the increment.

## Analysis

- **What is wrong:** a well-posed, exactly representable frictionless contact problem cannot be driven to its prescribed load once the meshes are finer than 2×2×2 / 3×3×2. The stopping factor does not improve with refinement, and line search can make it much worse (n = 4: 0.63 → 0.19). No converged solution exists at the final load, so the user gets an error rather than a wrong answer.
- **Likely locus (inference):** the nonlinear increment acceptance logic interacting with the contact constraint update on nonmatching interfaces. Candidates:
  - the "residual grows" rejection triggering on residual changes that are already below the force tolerance;
  - the energy criterion (1e-10) being unattainable once contact status or penalty enforcement changes between iterations;
  - contact active-set changes between iterations: more secondary nodes fall inside main faces as the mesh is refined, which would fit the earlier stops at larger n.
- **Suggested diagnostics:** report which convergence criterion failed and its value at each rejected iteration. Treat a residual that is "growing" but already below the force tolerance as converged when the displacement and energy criteria agree.
- **Not claimed:** that every solver setting fails, or a specific faulty routine.

## Suggested acceptance test

This uniform-compression contact patch test for n = 1…5. Assert that the full load is reached and that the nodal displacements match the affine solution within a penalty-consistent tolerance.

## Summary

In an **empty box** with a uniform velocity inlet, `slip` side faces and a convective pressure outlet, laminar TRT at τ = 0.505 (also 0.507) turns a 1e-6 transverse velocity disturbance into an exponentially growing mode. The mode starts on the inlet plane, and the stability guard trips at step 2838.

The same disturbance decays to round-off in each of these cases:
- the identical box with periodic side faces;
- a closed, fully periodic box;
- the regularized collision;
- τ = 0.512.

Without a disturbance the uniform stream is preserved exactly. The growth therefore comes from the inlet/slip-face boundary treatment, not from the physics or the bulk collision.

This is a body-free reduction of the inlet failure in #792, which reports a Re 100 sphere with TRT at τ = 0.518 going unstable at the velocity inlet.

Tested revision: `cb976117889edf8de5f093ba949980b0aa79c1e2`, CPU backend, f64, rustc 1.99.1.

## Why growth is wrong

The base state is uniform flow, ρ = 1, **u** = (0.05, 0, 0), in lattice units. It satisfies the velocity inlet, the slip side faces and the outlet exactly, and the zero-disturbance control keeps it at round-off for 30 000 steps.

The disturbance adds ±1e-6 to u_x on alternating y rows, a grid-scale checkerboard. In a viscous fluid at this Reynolds number (box Re = U·L/ν ≈ 480), uniform flow is linearly stable. A small disturbance should be advected and damped, and in the closed periodic box it is. Exponential growth starting at the inlet, at fixed viscosity and with no forcing, is a numerical instability of the boundary scheme.

## Reproduce

An 82-line program using only the public `fieldcraft-lbm` / `fieldcraft-model` API.

`Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[package]
name = "inlet-slip"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2024"

[workspace]

[dependencies]
fieldcraft-lbm = { git = "https://github.com/tangency-labs/fieldcraft", rev = "cb976117889edf8de5f093ba949980b0aa79c1e2" }
fieldcraft-model = { git = "https://github.com/tangency-labs/fieldcraft", rev = "cb976117889edf8de5f093ba949980b0aa79c1e2" }
serde_json = "1"
```

`src/main.rs`:

```rust
//! Empty box: uniform velocity inlet at x-min, convective pressure outlet at x-max, and `slip` or
//! `periodic` y/z faces (`closed`: every face periodic, no inlet or outlet). The uniform stream
//! gets a +-seed checkerboard on u_x, alternating along y (or along x). Prints max|rho-1| on
//! the inlet plane x=0, on x=1, x=2 and the largest over x>=3.
//! Usage: inlet-slip <trt|regularized> <tau> <slip|periodic|closed> <seed> [y|x] [NXxNYxNZ=16x16x16]
use fieldcraft_lbm::{Flow, Limits, Setup};
use fieldcraft_model::{Collision, Fluid, FluidDomain, Material, Model, Procedure, Step};
use serde_json::json;

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error + Send + Sync>> {
    let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();
    let collision: Collision = serde_json::from_value(json!(args[1]))?;
    let tau: f64 = args[2].parse()?;
    let sides = args[3].as_str();
    let seed: f64 = args[4].parse()?;
    let axis = if args.get(5).map(String::as_str) == Some("x") { 0 } else { 1 };
    let box_size = args.get(6).map_or("16x16x16", String::as_str);
    let n: Vec<usize> = box_size.split('x').map(str::parse).collect::<Result<_, _>>()?;
    let (speed, steps) = (0.05, 30_000u64);

    let side = json!({"type": if sides == "slip" { "slip" } else { "periodic" }});
    let mut faces = json!({"x_min": {"type": "velocity", "velocity": [speed, 0., 0.], "profile": "uniform"},
        "x_max": {"type": "pressure", "pressure": 0., "outflow": "convective"},
        "y_min": side, "y_max": side, "z_min": side, "z_max": side});
    if sides == "closed" {
        faces["x_min"] = side.clone();
        faces["x_max"] = side.clone();
    }
    let domain: FluidDomain = serde_json::from_value(json!({"material": "Fluid",
        "min": [0., 0., 0.], "max": n, "spacing": 1., "faces": faces,
        "initial_velocity": [speed, 0., 0.]}))?;

    let mut model = Model::default();
    model.add_material("Fluid", Material {
        density: Some(1.),
        fluid: Some(Fluid::Newtonian { viscosity: (tau - 0.5) / 3. }),
        ..Material::default()
    })?;
    model.add_fluid_domain("Domain", domain)?;
    model.add_step(Step::new("Run", Procedure::Fluid {
        domain: "Domain".into(), end_time: None, steps: Some(steps), time_step: Some(1.),
        frames: 1, magic: None, sigma: None, collision: Some(collision), turbulence: None,
        steady: None,
    }), None)?;

    let setup = Setup::new(&model, None, &[], &Limits::default())?;
    assert!((setup.tau_plus - tau).abs() < 1e-12);
    let mut flow = Flow::new(&setup, &[])?;
    let grid = *flow.grid();
    flow.set_equilibrium(|cell| {
        let sign = if grid.coords(cell)[axis] % 2 == 0 { 1. } else { -1. };
        (1., [speed + seed * sign, 0., 0.])
    });

    // max |rho - 1| on x = 0, 1, 2 and over x >= 3
    let planes = |flow: &Flow| {
        let mut d = [0f64; 4];
        for cell in 0..grid.len() {
            if let Some((rho, _)) = flow.moments(cell) {
                let x = grid.coords(cell)[0].min(3);
                d[x] = d[x].max((rho - 1.).abs());
            }
        }
        d
    };
    let mut worst = 0f64;
    for step in 1..=steps {
        if let Err(e) = flow.step() {
            println!("step {step}: {e}");
            std::process::exit(1);
        }
        let d = planes(&flow);
        worst = d.iter().fold(worst, |a, &b| a.max(b));
        if step % 500 == 0 {
            println!("step {step:5}: max|rho-1| x=0 {:.1e}  x=1 {:.1e}  x=2 {:.1e}  x>=3 {:.1e}",
                d[0], d[1], d[2], d[3]);
        }
    }
    println!("completed {steps} steps, largest departure {worst:.1e}, final {:.1e}",
        planes(&flow).iter().fold(0f64, |a, &b| a.max(b)));
    Ok(())
}
```

```sh
cargo run --release -- trt 0.505 slip 1e-6
```

Output:

```text
step   500: max|rho-1| x=0 5.4e-8  x=1 7.2e-8  x=2 5.3e-8  x>=3 8.4e-8
step  1000: max|rho-1| x=0 4.0e-7  x=1 3.3e-7  x=2 1.2e-7  x>=3 9.4e-8
step  1500: max|rho-1| x=0 5.6e-6  x=1 3.0e-6  x=2 5.2e-7  x>=3 3.0e-7
step  2000: max|rho-1| x=0 7.0e-5  x=1 3.7e-5  x=2 6.1e-6  x>=3 3.3e-6
step  2500: max|rho-1| x=0 8.9e-4  x=1 5.5e-4  x=2 1.3e-4  x>=3 4.9e-5
step 2838: unstable: the flow went unstable at time step 2838: the lattice speed reached 0.523 (limit 0.5) or a density stopped being a positive number; lower the time step, refine the spacing or check the boundary conditions
```

`Setup::new` accepts the input without warnings or overrides, and τ = 0.505 is above the setup floor.

## Expected vs actual

Disturbed cases, one change each against `trt 0.505 slip 1e-6` (16×16×16 box). Every one should complete 30 000 steps with the disturbance decaying. Each instead ends with `the flow went unstable at time step N: the lattice speed reached … (limit 0.5) or a density stopped being a positive number`.

| Arguments | Change | Guard trips at step |
|---|---|---:|
| `trt 0.505 slip 1e-6` | — | 2838 |
| `trt 0.505 slip -1e-6` | disturbance sign flipped | 2838 |
| `trt 0.505 slip 1e-4` | 100× larger disturbance | 1923 |
| `trt 0.505 slip 1e-6 y 8x16x16` | half the length | 2865 |
| `trt 0.505 slip 1e-6 y 32x16x16` | double the length | 2837 |
| `trt 0.505 slip 1e-6 y 16x32x32` | double the cross-section | 3050 |
| `trt 0.505 slip 1e-6 y 16x8x8` | half the cross-section | 3800 |
| `trt 0.507 slip 1e-6` | τ = 0.507 | 12217 |

Controls, all completing 30 000 steps. The largest departure includes the imposed disturbance itself at step 1.

| Arguments | Change | Largest max\|ρ − 1\| | Final max\|ρ − 1\| |
|---|---|---:|---:|
| `trt 0.505 slip 0` | no disturbance | 3.7e-14 | 1.0e-14 |
| `trt 0.505 periodic 1e-6` | periodic y/z faces | 1.3e-6 | 2.4e-14 |
| `trt 0.505 closed 1e-6` | all faces periodic, no inlet/outlet | 2.6e-13 | 2.6e-13 |
| `regularized 0.505 slip 1e-6` | regularized collision | 1.1e-6 | 1.1e-13 |
| `trt 0.512 slip 1e-6` | τ = 0.512 | 1.6e-6 | 1.0e-14 |
| `trt 0.505 slip 1e-6 x` | disturbance alternates along x instead of y | 8.4e-7 | 1.2e-10 |

### Where the mode lives

- In the output above, the inlet plane x = 0 grows at about 0.005 per step (×12.5 every 500 steps). The departure decreases monotonically downstream: x = 0 > x = 1 > x = 2 > x ≥ 3.
- When the inlet plane first passes 1e-4, it exceeds the largest departure on planes x ≥ 3 by:
  - **21×** at the 16×16, 32×32 cross-sections and at lengths 8 and 32;
  - **15×** at τ = 0.507;
  - **7×** in the narrow 8×8 cross-section. There the mode still starts at x = 0 but penetrates relatively further.
- The 100× larger disturbance fails 915 steps earlier. A linear mode growing at 0.005 per step predicts ln(100)/0.005 ≈ 920 steps.
- The sign of the disturbance does not matter, and the domain length barely changes the failure step. Both fit a mode attached to the inlet plane.

## Analysis

- **What is wrong:** the velocity-inlet boundary, together with slip side faces under TRT at low τ, feeds energy into a transverse grid-scale mode on the inlet plane.
  - The bulk scheme damps the same disturbance (closed box).
  - The inlet/outlet pair is neutral when the side faces are periodic.
  - The most likely locus is the interaction of the inlet reconstruction with the slip faces: the inlet edges and corners, and the rows next to them. This is inferred from the controls, not located in the code.
- **Dependence on τ:** the mode grows at 0.505 and 0.507 and decays at 0.512 in this box.
  - In the Re 100 sphere of #792 (τ = 0.518, 12 cells per diameter), the same inlet-plane growth appears, driven by the body's disturbance field instead of an imposed one.
  - At 8 cells per diameter (τ = 0.512) the sphere fails about twice as early in physical time, with a 3× faster per-step growth rate.
- **Why the regularized operator survives:** as in #792, it suppresses the mode.
- **Not claimed:** a specific faulty routine, or that every slip-sided inlet is unstable at every τ.

## Suggested acceptance test

An empty box with velocity inlet, slip sides and convective outlet; TRT; τ ∈ {0.5005, 0.505, 0.518}; a 1e-6 transverse checkerboard. Assert that the maximum departure after 30 000 steps does not exceed its initial value.

Later optimisation, once #399 (projection blocking) and #398 (relaxation) are in and meshes reach millions of nodes.

**GPU-friendly steps** (per node / per element, no global solve):
- transfinite interpolation filling the blocks;
- elliptic smoothing (Winslow / Thomas–Middlecoff) — Jacobi-style sweeps, the dominant cost on big meshes;
- projection onto analytic surfaces (plane, cylinder, sphere, cone, torus); free-form faces stay on the CPU kernel;
- quality metrics per element;
- node relaxation (#398) with a node colouring so neighbours never move in the same pass.

**Stays on the CPU:** block planning, interval assignment, the provenance plan (#402), B-rep queries on free-form surfaces.

**How:** `wgpu` compute shaders (already a dependency for rendering), behind the same API with a CPU fallback (results identical within tolerance; tests compare both). Must not break wasm (use WebGPU there or the CPU path).

**Worth it when:** smoothing/projection time exceeds a few seconds — benchmark first on a 1M-node block mesh and report CPU vs GPU.
